## Running the PoC

```bash
# User-data mode: place your input file in sample_input/ first
python run_poc.py

# Synthetic-data mode (generates sample input for document/RAG patterns;
# audio patterns cannot be auto-generated -- place a real audio file in sample_input/):
python run_poc.py --synthetic
```

Results are written to `output/`. The run prints counts, field names and file names,
not the extracted values; add `--verbose` to print them as well. (A RAG PoC's interactive
mode shows each answer, since you asked the question there; `--query` prints the answer
only with `--verbose`.) An error is printed as one short line; add `--debug` for the full
traceback.

The files in `output/` hold the extracted content as plain, unencrypted files. Who may
read them and how long they are kept is the owner's decision: delete them when they are
no longer needed, above all when the inputs hold personal data.

Every stage in `config.yaml` names its provider. If one is removed, the run stops,
unless the blueprint set `models.allow_provider_fallback: true`; then the run warns and
uses the top-level `provider`. When the blueprint allows no external model API
(`governance` in `config.yaml`), a stage that would call one stops the run before any
credential is read, and audio transcripts are not sent for enhancement.

